      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T14:37:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is equivalent to:\n\n```\nif (dstBlendMode) {\n    // The src branch doesn\u0027t matter at all, so don\u0027t even traverse it\n    AddPrimitiveColor(childCOntext, keyBuilder, gatherer, fSkipColorXform);\n} else {\n    Blend(childContext, keyBuilder, gatherer,\n        /* addBlendToKey\u003d */ [\u0026] () -\u003e void {\n            AddToKey(keyContext, keyBuilder, gatherer, fPrimitiveBlender.get());\n        },\n        /* addSrcToKey\u003d */ [\u0026] () -\u003e void {\n            this-\u003eaddPaintColorToKey(keyContext, keyBuilder, gatherer);\n        },\n        /* addDstToKey\u003d */ [\u0026] () -\u003e void {\n            AddPrimitiveColor(...);\n        });\n}\n```\n\nThis would produce the tighter key of just `PrimitiveColor` vs. `Blend [ Passthrough PrimitiveColor Dst ]`, but you\u0027re right that SkSL inlining should mean that everything works out to the same instructions.  And since we switch to Passthrough for the `addSrcToKey`, we aren\u0027t including the unnecessary src elements in the key that would create new pipelines.\n\nUp to you which you think is more readable, but if you keep the original structure, I would flip the condition and if/else cases in the `addSrcToKey` around so both src and dst have `if (dstBlendMode)` and add a comment in the src\u0027s passthrough case saying it doesn\u0027t matter what the src would be so just use prior output.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 202,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 219,
        "endChar": 7
      },
      "revId": "730cf9ea72ad36f075663210539202f47cc5ccab",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
